---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import TableWrap from '../components/TableWrap.astro';

const cssExample = `/* Usage in components */
.dashboard-sidebar {
  width: var(--nav-width);
  background: var(--color-surface);
  border-right: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  padding: var(--spacing-4);
}

.data-table td {
  padding: calc(var(--spacing-3) * var(--density-factor));
  font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
  color: var(--color-foreground);
}`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Design Discovery" sectionLabel="Design Discovery" activeSlug="design-discovery">

  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <span class="section-label">Design Discovery</span>
    <h2>How VibeCrew Creates a Design System Tailored to Your Product</h2>
    <p>Design Discovery replaces the generic &ldquo;pick a color&rdquo; approach with a <strong>3-phase contextual interview</strong> that understands what you're building, who it's for, and how it should feel. The result is a design system that encodes real product understanding &mdash; not just aesthetic preferences.</p>

    <p>Design Discovery runs during <code>/new-project</code> as <strong>Tier 1 Step 2</strong>, between VISION.md (goals) and the TDR (technology decisions). It produces two artifacts:</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon purple">CSS</div>
        <h4>design-system.css</h4>
        <p>CSS custom properties for colors, typography, spacing, radii, shadows, transitions, and layout context. Every component references these tokens.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-icon blue">MD</div>
        <h4>design-brief.md</h4>
        <p>The &ldquo;why&rdquo; document. Captures product context, visual direction rationale, component preferences, and design principles for agents to reference.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">VISION.md</span> <span class="muted">&rarr;</span> <span class="highlight">Design Discovery</span> <span class="muted">&rarr;</span> <span class="highlight">TDR</span> <span class="muted">&rarr;</span> <span class="highlight">Roadmap</span> <span class="muted">&rarr;</span> <span class="highlight">Diagrams</span> <span class="muted">&rarr;</span> <span class="highlight">CLAUDE.md</span>
<span class="muted">           Phase 1: Context     &uarr;</span>
<span class="muted">           Phase 2: Direction   &uarr; Tier 1 Foundation</span>
<span class="muted">           Phase 3: Components  &uarr;</span></div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="phase-1">
    <span class="section-label">Phase 1</span>
    <h2>Product &amp; Audience Context</h2>
    <p>Six questions establish who you're building for, what problem you're solving, and how the product should feel. Every question offers predefined options <strong>and</strong> a free-text alternative &mdash; you're never locked into the provided choices.</p>

    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>#</th><th>Question</th><th>Options</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Q1</td>
            <td><strong>What are we building?</strong></td>
            <td>SaaS dashboard &middot; Marketplace / e-commerce &middot; Content platform &middot; Mobile-first tool &middot; Social / community &middot; Creative tool &middot; <em>or describe it</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Q2</td>
            <td><strong>Who uses it?</strong></td>
            <td>Technical professionals &middot; Non-technical consumers &middot; Enterprise teams &middot; Creative freelancers &middot; Students / learners &middot; <em>or describe them</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Q3</td>
            <td><strong>What problem does it solve for them?</strong></td>
            <td>Saves time on repetitive tasks &middot; Reduces complexity / simplifies decisions &middot; Enables collaboration across teams &middot; Provides insights from data &middot; Replaces manual / offline processes &middot; <em>or describe the problem</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Q4</td>
            <td><strong>How do they use it?</strong></td>
            <td>Quick daily checks &middot; Deep focused sessions &middot; On-the-go mobile &middot; Collaborative team use &middot; Passive consumption &middot; <em>or describe the usage</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Q5</td>
            <td><strong>How should it feel?</strong></td>
            <td>Trustworthy &amp; professional &middot; Playful &amp; energetic &middot; Minimal &amp; calm &middot; Bold &amp; premium &middot; Warm &amp; approachable &middot; <em>or describe the feeling</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Q6</td>
            <td><strong>What is the primary thing users do?</strong></td>
            <td>Analyze data &middot; Create content &middot; Manage workflows &middot; Browse &amp; discover &middot; Communicate &middot; Transact &middot; <em>or describe the action</em></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>Free-Text Interpretation</h3>
    <p>When you type your own answer instead of picking an option, VibeCrew interprets it, maps it to design parameters, and confirms before proceeding.</p>
    <Callout type="tip" title="Example">
      You type: &ldquo;busy nurses checking patient vitals between rounds&rdquo;<br>
      VibeCrew interprets: <strong>quick daily checks</strong> + <strong>trustworthy &amp; professional</strong> + <strong>analyze data</strong>, and confirms this mapping with you before moving on.
    </Callout>

    <p>After all 6 answers, a summary is presented:</p>
    <Callout type="info">
      &ldquo;We're building a <strong>SaaS dashboard</strong> for <strong>enterprise teams</strong> that <strong>provides insights from data</strong>, designed for <strong>deep focused sessions</strong>. It should feel <strong>trustworthy &amp; professional</strong>, focused on <strong>analyzing data</strong>.&rdquo;
    </Callout>

    <h3>Profile Adaptations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Minimal verbosity</strong>: Options only, no explanations of why each question matters</li>
      <li><strong>Educational verbosity</strong>: Explains the UX principle behind each question before asking</li>
      <li><strong>Supervised autonomy</strong>: Confirms the summary before proceeding to Phase 2</li>
      <li><strong>Full-auto autonomy</strong>: Skips the interview entirely. Uses VISION.md to infer answers, picks Direction A, and generates both files immediately</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="phase-2">
    <span class="section-label">Phase 2</span>
    <h2>Visual Direction</h2>
    <p>Based on your Phase 1 answers, VibeCrew generates <strong>3 tailored visual directions</strong>. These aren't generic &mdash; a &ldquo;SaaS dashboard that should feel trustworthy&rdquo; gets very different options than a &ldquo;mobile tool that should feel playful.&rdquo;</p>

    <h3>The Seed Matrix</h3>
    <p>A reference matrix of known product aesthetics gives the AI creative starting points. The actual directions are adapted and blended based on your specific combination of answers.</p>

    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Product + Feel</th><th>Direction A (Safe)</th><th>Direction B (Modern)</th><th>Direction C (Signature)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Dashboard + Professional</td><td>Tableau / Datadog</td><td>Linear / Vercel</td><td>Stripe Dashboard</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dashboard + Minimal</td><td>Plausible Analytics</td><td>Raycast</td><td>Arc Browser</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Marketplace + Energetic</td><td>Shopify</td><td>Gumroad</td><td>Product Hunt</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Content + Calm</td><td>Medium</td><td>Notion</td><td>iA Writer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mobile + Playful</td><td>Duolingo</td><td>Headspace</td><td>Cash App</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Social + Energetic</td><td>Discord</td><td>Threads</td><td>BeReal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Creative + Bold</td><td>Figma</td><td>Framer</td><td>Runway</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h3>What Each Direction Includes</h3>
    <p>For each of the 3 directions, VibeCrew generates:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Name</strong> &mdash; 2-3 words (e.g., &ldquo;Linear Finance&rdquo;)</li>
      <li><strong>Reference</strong> &mdash; 1-2 known products for inspiration</li>
      <li><strong>Color palette</strong> &mdash; Primary HSL, neutral temperature, accent approach</li>
      <li><strong>Typography</strong> &mdash; Heading + body font pairing</li>
      <li><strong>Spacing/density</strong> &mdash; Compact, comfortable, or spacious</li>
      <li><strong>Border radius</strong> &mdash; Sharp, subtle, rounded, or pill</li>
      <li><strong>Shadow strategy</strong> &mdash; Flat, subtle, layered, or dramatic</li>
      <li><strong>One-line vibe</strong> &mdash; A sentence capturing the feel</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The three directions are presented as a compact comparison, and you choose: <strong>A</strong>, <strong>B</strong>, <strong>C</strong>, or describe your own direction in free text. If you describe a custom direction, VibeCrew generates the full specification and confirms before proceeding.</p>

    <h3>tweakcn Theme Presets (optional)</h3>
    <p>After you pick a direction, VibeCrew offers matching <strong>tweakcn</strong> theme presets as optional starting points for design token values. Based on your chosen direction's color temperature, density, and shadow style, it suggests 2 relevant presets &mdash; or you can start from scratch or skip entirely.</p>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Example Directions">
      For &ldquo;SaaS dashboard + trustworthy &amp; professional&rdquo;, you might see:<br>
      <strong>A: Bloomberg Modern</strong> &mdash; Dense, data-first, monospace headings, cool neutrals<br>
      <strong>B: Linear Finance</strong> &mdash; Clean geometry, Inter + JetBrains Mono, subtle shadows<br>
      <strong>C: Stripe Dashboard</strong> &mdash; Premium purple accents, generous spacing, layered depth
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="phase-3">
    <span class="section-label">Phase 3</span>
    <h2>Component Preferences</h2>
    <p>The final phase asks 2-3 questions about component patterns, <strong>filtered by your Phase 1 answers</strong>. A dashboard gets different navigation options than a content platform.</p>

    <h4>Q7: Navigation Style (always asked)</h4>
    <p>Options use precise component names internally while showing plain language to you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Dashboards</strong>: Sidebar with collapsible sections (Sidebar) / top navigation bar (Navigation Menu) / combined</li>
      <li><strong>Content &amp; marketplace</strong>: Top navigation bar (Navigation Menu) / hamburger slide-out (Sheet) / bottom tab bar (Tabs)</li>
      <li><strong>Mobile-first</strong>: Bottom tab bar (Tabs) / hamburger slide-out (Sheet) / gesture-based</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Q8: Data Display (conditional)</h4>
    <p>Only asked if your primary action involves data, workflows, or transactions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Dense sortable tables with filters (Data Table) / card grid with previews (Card grid) / compact list with expandable details (Accordion) / mixed: summary cards + detailed table below</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Q9: Interaction Density (always asked)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Minimal</strong> &mdash; Lots of whitespace, one action per view</li>
      <li><strong>Moderate</strong> &mdash; Balanced density, grouped actions</li>
      <li><strong>Dense</strong> &mdash; Information-rich, multiple actions visible (Data Table, Sidebar, Tabs)</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Q10: Feedback Style (always asked)</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Subtle</strong> &mdash; Small toasts for confirmations, inline validation (Sonner + inline errors)</li>
      <li><strong>Conversational</strong> &mdash; Confirmation dialogs for important actions (Alert Dialog + Sonner)</li>
      <li><strong>Minimal</strong> &mdash; No confirmations, undo-based (Optimistic UI + undo Sonner)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>These preferences directly inform the <strong>Builder agent</strong> during Tier 2 feature development. When creating components, the Builder reads both the design brief and the <a href="/ui-patterns">component reference</a> to select the right component for each UI need, apply interaction patterns (like Optimistic UI or Skeleton Loading), and verify keyboard accessibility.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="output">
    <span class="section-label">Output</span>
    <h2>Output Artifacts</h2>

    <h3>design-system.css</h3>
    <p>A complete set of CSS custom properties on <code>:root</code>. Token categories:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Colors</strong> &mdash; HSL-based primary, secondary, neutral, and semantic colors with dark mode overrides. When shadcn/ui is the expected component library (default), all 46 shadcn CSS tokens are generated using OKLCH color space for seamless component installation</li>
      <li><strong>Typography</strong> &mdash; Modular scale (1.25 ratio), font families, weights, line heights</li>
      <li><strong>Spacing</strong> &mdash; 4px base unit, <code>--spacing-1</code> through <code>--spacing-24</code></li>
      <li><strong>Border radius</strong> &mdash; Scale from <code>--radius-sm</code> to <code>--radius-full</code></li>
      <li><strong>Shadows</strong> &mdash; Layered from <code>--shadow-xs</code> to <code>--shadow-xl</code></li>
      <li><strong>Transitions</strong> &mdash; Fast (150ms), normal (250ms), slow (350ms)</li>
      <li><strong>Layout context</strong> &mdash; <code>--density-factor</code>, <code>--content-width-max</code>, <code>--nav-width</code> (from Design Discovery)</li>
    </ul>

    <CodeBlock code={cssExample} lang="css" label="CSS" />

    <h3>design-brief.md</h3>
    <p>The &ldquo;why&rdquo; document that captures every decision and its rationale. Sections include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Product Context</strong> &mdash; Category, audience, core problem, usage pattern, emotional direction, key action</li>
      <li><strong>Visual Direction</strong> &mdash; Chosen direction name, reference products, rationale</li>
      <li><strong>Design Tokens Summary</strong> &mdash; Key tokens with reasoning for each choice</li>
      <li><strong>Component Preferences</strong> &mdash; Navigation, data display, interaction density with rationale</li>
      <li><strong>Design Principles</strong> &mdash; 3 principles derived from context</li>
      <li><strong>Accessibility Baseline</strong> &mdash; WCAG AA, focus indicators, touch targets, reduced motion</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="customization">
    <span class="section-label">Customization</span>
    <h2>Customization &amp; Iteration</h2>

    <h3>The Approve / Edit / Skip Flow</h3>
    <p>After both files are generated, VibeCrew presents a summary and asks: <em>&ldquo;Does this design direction feel right?&rdquo;</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Approve</strong> &mdash; Lock in the design system and proceed to TDR</li>
      <li><strong>Edit</strong> &mdash; Specify what to change (colors, fonts, density, etc.) and VibeCrew revises</li>
      <li><strong>Skip</strong> &mdash; Mark as skipped and move on (you can always come back)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Extending During Tier 2</h3>
    <p>The design system is not static. During feature development, the Builder agent can <strong>add</strong> new tokens (e.g., a feature-specific accent color) but cannot <strong>modify</strong> existing tokens without developer approval. This preserves consistency across the application while allowing organic growth.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <section class="section" id="agent-context">
    <Callout type="info" title="Design Brief as Agent Context">
      The <code>design-brief.md</code> file gives the Builder agent <strong>product awareness</strong> beyond raw design tokens. When creating a new component, the Builder reads both the design brief and the <strong>component reference</strong> to understand:<br><br>
      &bull; <strong>Navigation style</strong> &mdash; Should this page use a Sidebar or Navigation Menu?<br>
      &bull; <strong>Data display</strong> &mdash; Should data render as Data Tables, Card grids, or Accordions?<br>
      &bull; <strong>Interaction density</strong> &mdash; How much whitespace between elements?<br>
      &bull; <strong>Audience context</strong> &mdash; Are users technical or non-technical? Quick glances or deep sessions?<br>
      &bull; <strong>Interaction patterns</strong> &mdash; Should mutations use Optimistic UI? Should data-fetching sections show Skeleton Loading?<br>
      &bull; <strong>Keyboard &amp; focus</strong> &mdash; Does every overlay trap focus? Does every interactive element have a visible focus ring?<br><br>
      This transforms the Builder from &ldquo;apply these tokens&rdquo; to &ldquo;design for this audience with precise components, smart interaction patterns, and full keyboard accessibility.&rdquo;
    </Callout>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
